---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { MATERIALS } from '../data/materials';
import { MACHINES } from '../data/machines';
import { GLOSSARY } from '../data/glossary';
import { SITE } from '../site';

const featuredMaterials = MATERIALS.slice(0, 6);
---

<Base
  title="Nylon3DP — The Nylon 3D Printing Reference: PA11, PA12, SLS, MJF"
  description="The nylon 3D printing reference: PA11/PA12 datasheet database, SLS, MJF, SAF and FDM processes, machine specs and guides — every number manufacturer-sourced."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>The Nylon 3D Printing Reference</h1>
    <p class="lede">Nylon is the engineering polymer that 3D printing actually builds with — and its data is scattered across dozens of manufacturer datasheets. Nylon3DP gathers it in one place: material properties, machine specifications, process explainers and design guides for PA11, PA12, PA6 and filled nylons, with every number traced to the manufacturer's own documentation.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="quick-answer">
    <p class="qa-label">Quick answer — what is nylon 3D printing?</p>
    <p>Nylon (polyamide) 3D printing means producing parts from PA12, PA11, PA6 or filled nylon grades, mainly by powder-bed fusion — <a href="/processes/sls/">SLS</a> and <a href="/processes/mjf/">MJF</a> — or by extruding nylon filament (<a href="/processes/fdm/">FDM</a>). Printed nylon is tough, wear-resistant and moderately heat-resistant, which makes it the default choice for functional end-use parts: hinges, clips, gears, ducts, brackets, jigs and orthotics. PA12 is the standard grade; PA11 is the ductile, bio-based alternative.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Start with the databases</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <a class="card" href="/materials/">
      <h3>Material database</h3>
      <p>{MATERIALS.length} nylon grades with full datasheet tables — powders from EOS, Formlabs, HP and BASF, filaments from Polymaker and Bambu Lab.</p>
      <p class="meta">PA12 · PA11 · PA6 · glass-filled · PA-CF · CoPA</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/machines/">
      <h3>Machine database</h3>
      <p>{MACHINES.length} powder-bed systems with manufacturer-verified build volumes and layer specs.</p>
      <p class="meta">HP Jet Fusion · EOS Formiga · Formlabs Fuse · Stratasys H350</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/processes/">
      <h3>Processes</h3>
      <p>How SLS, MJF, SAF and nylon FDM work, which materials each uses, and where each fits — plus the sourced SLS vs MJF comparison.</p>
      <p class="meta">SLS · MJF · SAF · FDM</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/guides/">
      <h3>Guides</h3>
      <p>PA11 vs PA12 decided on datasheet numbers; drying nylon filament at manufacturer settings; designing parts for SLS &amp; MJF from official design guides.</p>
      <p class="meta">3 guides at launch, growing</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/glossary/">
      <h3>Glossary</h3>
      <p>{GLOSSARY.length} definitions — refresh ratio, glass-filled, hygroscopic, HDT and the rest of the vocabulary the datasheets assume.</p>
      <p class="meta">Plain-language, cross-checked against the database</p>
    </a>
    <a class="card" href="/compare/sls-vs-mjf/">
      <h3>SLS vs MJF</h3>
      <p>The two production powder processes compared on their manufacturers' own PA12 data: strength, isotropy, colour, materials and economics.</p>
      <p class="meta">Comparison</p>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>Featured materials</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    {featuredMaterials.map((m) => (
      <a class="card" href={`/materials/${m.slug}/`}>
        <h3>{m.name}</h3>
        <p class="meta">{m.manufacturer} · {m.basePolymer} · {m.process}</p>
        <p>{m.quickAnswer.split('.')[0]}.</p>
      </a>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>Coverage so far — stated plainly</h2>
  <div class="note">
    <p>This is v1 of the reference, published November 2026: {MATERIALS.length} materials, {MACHINES.length} machines, 3 process deep-dives plus a comparison, 3 guides and {GLOSSARY.length} glossary terms. The goal is the complete nylon landscape — every significant powder, filament and machine, every major application area — built in verified stages. What is not yet covered is not guessed at: it is queued. The standard for every page is in the <a href="/editorial/">editorial and sourcing policy</a>.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Why trust the numbers here?</h2>
  <p>Because each one names its source. Property tables reproduce manufacturer datasheets (EOS, Formlabs, HP, BASF, Polymaker, Bambu Lab) with the test method kept next to the value; machine specs come from manufacturers' specification documents; design minimums come from official design guides. If a manufacturer does not publish a value, the table omits it rather than filling the gap. Corrections are welcome at <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.</p>
  <p class="updated">Last updated: {SITE.lastUpdated}</p>
</Base>
